,{"date":"2026-11-04","scheduled":true,"state":"REDUCE","depletion_start_mm":2.907,"baseline_applied_mm":5.0,"recommended_applied_mm":4.845000000000001,"depletion_end_mm":0.7224999999999993}],"assumptions":["Simulated, not your confirmed actions: every day in the window is replayed by the engine, not recorded from anything you did.","The baseline is your saved schedule applied in full on every scheduled day; schedule days are spread evenly from your last watering date.","The replay uses the same engine, cited constants, skip threshold, efficiency and application cap as a live recommendation.","A backtest has no historical forecast, so each scheduled day is decided from the depletion at the start of that day instead of a projected horizon.","The window starts at the refill point, the same convention the live recommendation uses before a recorded watering.","Rain and ET₀ are observed gridded Open-Meteo values for your coordinates, not measurements in your yard.","Estimated water avoided = baseline volume − simulated recommended volume. It is an estimate, not a metered saving.","GreenFlow provides irrigation advice. It does not control or modify your irrigation timer."],"limitations":["Observed weather only removes water the engine would have skipped; it cannot replay a forecast the way the live page does.","The schedule is your reported frequency, not a log of when the sprinkler ran.","No flow meter, water bill, or soil-moisture sensor is used.","Every scheduled day is decided from the water already short at the start of that day, so the simulated run never applies water in advance. A live recommendation projects a horizon instead and would apply more, which makes this avoided volume an upper bound rather than a typical week."],"sources":[{"id":"open-meteo-history","label":"Weather history used","citation":"Open-Meteo forecast API, daily past_days: observed precipitation_sum and et0_fao_evapotranspiration (FAO-56 reference evapotranspiration).","url":"https://open-meteo.com/"},{"id":"fao56-table-12","label":"Kc","citation":"FAO-56 Table 12, p. 112: cool-season turfgrass Kc mid 0.95; warm-season turfgrass Kc mid 0.85 (footnote 15).","url":"https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e0b.htm"},{"id":"fao56-table-22","label":"Depletion fraction","citation":"FAO-56 Table 22, p. 165: turfgrass depletion fraction p = 0.40 (cool season) and p = 0.50 (warm season).","url":"https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e0e.htm"},{"id":"fao56-table-19","label":"Soil water","citation":"FAO-56 Table 19, p. 144: field capacity and wilting point ranges; midpoints are used for each supported soil texture.","url":"https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e00.htm"},{"id":"penn-state-root-depth","label":"Root depth","citation":"Penn State Extension, The Cool-Season Turfgrasses: rooting depth is usually between 2 and 6 inches","url":"https://extension.psu.edu/the-cool-season-turfgrasses-basic-structures-growth-and-development/"},{"id":"csu-efficiency","label":"Irrigation efficiency","citation":"Colorado State University Extension, Drip Irrigation for Home Gardens: sprinkler systems are 50 to 70 percent efficient","url":"https://extension.colostate.edu/resource/drip-irrigation-for-home-gardens/"}]}